  DocPage(
    id: 'publish',
    title: 'Publish, managed components and plugins',
    summary: 'What happens to documents after they are written: a published app\'s model (its layouts, components, theme and identity), page paths and titles, components published by reference, managed component releases and hosted plugins.',
    when: 'What happens to documents after they are written: a published app\'s model (its layouts, components, theme and\nidentity), page paths and titles, components published by reference, managed component releases and hosted\nplugins. Read it when you set a canvas\'s URL path or title, or ship components others reuse; the documents\nthemselves do not change.',
    example: 'A page whose canvas has a URL path and a name (its default title):\n\n```json\n{\n  "schema": 3,\n  "id": "pricing",\n  "roots": ["pricing"],\n  "nodes": {\n    "pricing": {\n      "type": "canvas",\n      "name": "Pricing",\n      "path": "pricing",\n      "slots": {"body": "title"},\n      "layout": {"width": 390, "height": 200},\n      "decoration": {"fills": [{"color": "#FFFFFFFF"}]}\n    },\n    "title": {\n      "type": "text",\n      "layout": {"width": {"fill": 390}, "height": {"hug": 34}, "margin": 24},\n      "props": {"text": "Pricing", "style": {"fontSize": 28, "fontWeight": 700, "color": "#FF111827"}}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```',
    pitfalls: '- The publish model is written by the editor and the publish tools, not by hand in a page document.\n- A component\'s file must be named after its type.',
    words: 672,
    items: [],
    sections: [
      DocSection('publish-the-publish-model-s-parts', 'The publish model\'s parts', '| Class | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `PublishModel` | A published project: its layouts (by id, loaded on their own), entry screen, fonts, theme and APIs. |\n| `PublishLayout` |  |\n| `PublishComponent` | One published component definition (`publish/{p}/components/{id}`): its `document` (a component document: one root, a `widget` declaration) with its version and digest. |\n| `PublishComponentInfo` | What a publish model knows of one published component without loading it: its version and digest (a cached copy with another digest is stale), when it was published and the fonts its text uses (loaded with the screens that call it). |\n| `AppIdentity` | A published app\'s identity (the publish model\'s `appIdentity`). JSON: the identity\'s own (see the file comment), with every key this version doesn\'t model kept in `extra`. |\n| `PageIdentity` | One page\'s (or screen\'s) title and description override, keyed in `AppIdentity.pages` by an editor page id, or a canvas or layout id. |\n| `StyleSheet` | A project\'s style sheet (`style_sheets/{projectId}`, `schema: 2`). |\n| `NamedTextStyle` | A named text style. |\n| `CanvasBreakpoint` | Which project breakpoint a canvas of a layout is the design for (DESIGN.md §5.3). A layout with several canvases (one screen, one canvas per breakpoint) shows the one whose switch point is the highest at or below the width it\'s shown at (see `CanvasBreakpointsForWidth.forWidth`). |\n| `PluginRef` | What a project uses of a hosted plugin: the channel it follows, an optional major pin, and the version (and its digest) the editor built and previewed with. JSON: `{"id": "codelessly.charts", "channel": "stable", "pin": 2, "version": 14, "semver": "2.3.1", "digest": "9f2c…"}`. |\n| `Automation` | One automation of a project. Its `when` and its actions read the app\'s values (variables, `auth`, `storage`, `data`), `\${event}` (`name`, `props`) and `\${usage}`, the per-user counters the runtime keeps on the device:<br><br>- `usage.launches`, `usage.sessions`, `usage.firstSeen`, `usage.lastSeen` (ms since epoch), `usage.isFirstLaunch`, `usage.isFirstSession`;<br><br>- `usage.events.<name>.count` and `.last`: every app event fired;<br><br>- `usage.automations.<id>.count`, `.last` and `.session` (runs this session).<br><br>`maxRuns`, `maxPerSession` and `everyDays` limit how often it runs ("once", "once per session", "at most every 7 days"). |'),
      DocSection('publish-a-page-s-url-and-title', 'A page\'s URL and title', 'A canvas\'s `"path"` is its URL (`/pricing`); without one it gets a path from its name. The app identity\'s title\ntemplate (`\${page.title} · \${app.name}`) makes each page\'s title; a page can override its title and description.'),
      DocSection('publish-components-by-reference', 'Components by reference', 'A published site carries its components beside its pages; an app loads the ones a screen calls with the screen,\ncaches them, and follows a republished one: every instance updates where it is. Nothing to write in the\ndocuments ([published components](components.md#components-published-components)).'),
    ],
  ),
  DocPage(
    id: 'edit-ops',
    title: 'Edit ops and streaming',
    summary: 'Building and changing a document with edit operations: what a model sends in a `ui_apply` tool call, what an app\'s `UiSession` applies while the model streams, the retry protocol for rejected ops, the tools\' schemas and the trust profiles that limit what a model may use.',
    when: 'Building and changing a document with edit operations: what a model sends in a `ui_apply` tool call, what an app\'s\n`UiSession` applies while the model streams, the retry protocol for rejected ops, the tools\' schemas and the trust\nprofiles that limit what a model may use.',
    example: 'The ops that build the base\'s smallest document, top-down, as a model streams them:\n\n```json\n{\n  "ops": [\n    {"op": "roots", "roots": ["home"]},\n    {\n      "op": "set",\n      "id": "home",\n      "node": {\n        "type": "canvas",\n        "name": "Home",\n        "slots": {"body": "page"},\n        "layout": {"width": 390, "height": 844},\n        "decoration": {"fills": [{"color": "#FFFFFFFF"}]}\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "op": "set",\n      "id": "page",\n      "node": {\n        "type": "column",\n        "children": ["title", "body"],\n        "layout": {"width": {"fill": 390}, "height": {"fill": 844}, "padding": 24},\n        "props": {"crossAxisAlignment": "start", "spacing": 12}\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "op": "set",\n      "id": "title",\n      "node": {\n        "type": "text",\n        "layout": {"width": {"fill": 342}, "height": {"hug": 34}},\n        "props": {"text": "Hello", "style": {"fontWeight": 700, "fontSize": 28, "color": "#FF111827"}}\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "op": "set",\n      "id": "body",\n      "node": {\n        "type": "text",\n        "layout": {"width": {"fill": 342}, "height": {"hug": 40}},\n        "props": {\n          "text": "A column with two texts, filling the screen\'s width.",\n          "style": {"fontSize": 15, "color": "#FF4B5563", "height": 1.4}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```',
    pitfalls: '* A `patch` is an RFC 7396 merge patch on one node, shaped like the node: objects merge, lists and values replace,\n  `null` deletes a key. It never changes `children`, `slots` or `type` (`structural_key_in_patch`): child lists\n  change only through `set` (with `parent`), `move` and `remove`.\n* A `set` of an id that exists replaces the node (the result says which ids it created). Replacing a node that had\n  children it no longer lists is `would_orphan`: keep them, move or remove them first, or patch instead.\n* Each op is checked on its own, against what it touched: its structure (one parent, no cycle, no canvas as a child,\n  a parent that takes children there), the trust profile, and the nodes\' fields (the strict validator of\n  [render](render.md#page-render) on the nodes the op created or changed, their parents and children). A rejected op changes nothing; the others\n  apply (with `"atomic": true`, all or none).\n* A batch may carry `"baseRev"`, the revision the author read: a patch of a field, or a set of a node, that another\n  author changed since is `stale_rev`, with the current value. Every applied batch has inverse ops: applied\n  atomically, they restore the document (undo).',
    words: 3297,
    items: [],
    sections: [
      DocSection('edit-ops-the-ops', 'The ops', 'A document is built and changed with six **edit operations**, each addressed by node id: what an agent sends in a\n`ui_apply` tool call, what `codelessly apply` takes, and what an app\'s `UiSession` applies while a model streams\n(`applyOps`, `decodeOps`, `OpStreamDecoder` in `package:codelessly_api/core.dart`). Generation is just the first\nbatch of ops; every change after it is a few more.\n\n| Op | Fields | Means | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `set` | `id`, `node`, `nodes`, `parent`, `index`, `slot`, `placeholders` | Create node `id` (or replace it: the result says which), optionally placed under `parent` at `index` (default: last) or in `slot`. `node` is the node as the document holds it under "nodes": its `children` may list ids that arrive in later ops (pending until then). `nodes` sets several nodes at once (id → node), in order. `placeholders` gives a size (a layout fragment) to show for a pending child until it arrives. | `{"op":"set","id":"faqTitle","parent":"faqSec","index":0,"node":{"type":"text","layout":{"width":{"hug":120},"height":{"hug":28}},"props":{"text":"Questions","style":{"fontSize":22,"fontWeight":700,"color":"#FF111827"}}}}` |\n| `patch` | `id` (required), `patch` (required) | Merge `patch` into node `id` (RFC 7396): objects merge, arrays and scalars replace, null deletes a key. Shaped like the node (`{"props": {"text": "Book a demo"}}`). It cannot change `children`, `slots` or `type`. | `{"op":"patch","id":"heroTitle","patch":{"props":{"text":"Book a demo","style":{"color":"#FF0F766E"}}}}` |\n| `move` | `id` (required), `parent` (required), `index`, `slot` | Move node `id` (with its subtree) under `parent` at `index` (default: last), or into `slot`. Reorder: move within the same parent. | `{"op":"move","id":"faqSec","parent":"page","index":2}` |\n| `remove` | `ids` (required) | Remove the nodes `ids` and everything under them; their parents stop listing them. | `{"op":"remove","ids":["promoBanner"]}` |\n| `vars` | `scope` (required), `set`, `remove` | Variables of `scope` (a canvas id, or "page_variables"): `set` maps a name to its new value, or to a full variable object (`{"type": "boolean", "value": false}`) to declare it; `remove` lists names to remove. Bindings that read them update at once. | `{"op":"vars","scope":"pricing","set":{"annual":true}}` |\n| `roots` | `roots` (required) | Set the document\'s canvases, in order. A root may name a canvas that arrives in a later op. | `{"op":"roots","roots":["home"]}` |\n| `scripts` | `set`, `remove` | The document\'s scripts (sandboxed JavaScript, where allowed): `set` maps a name to {"source", "params", "capabilities", "timeoutMs"}; `remove` lists names. A reaction runs one ({"type": "runScript", "script": name}), a formula calls it (script.name(...)). | `{"op":"scripts","set":{"clamp":{"params":["v"],"source":"return Math.max(0, Math.min(9, v));"}}}` |'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-codes-of-the-edit-layer', 'Codes of the edit layer', '| Code | Rejects the op | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `invalid_op` | yes | An op that is not an object, or names no known "op" (set, patch, move, remove, vars, roots, scripts). |\n| `missing_field` | yes | An op without a field it needs ("id", "node", "patch", "parent", "ids", "scope", "roots"). |\n| `wrong_type` | yes | A field of an op with the wrong JSON type (an id that is not text, a node that is not an object). |\n| `unknown_id` | yes | A patch, move or remove of a node the document does not have, or a set or move under a parent it does not have. |\n| `structural_key_in_patch` | yes | A patch that changes "children" or "slots" (or "type"): child lists change only through set, move and remove; a type through set. |\n| `stale_rev` | yes | The op changes a field another author changed after the batch\'s "baseRev": re-read the node (ui_read) and decide. |\n| `would_orphan` | yes | A set that replaces a node and drops children it had: they would be left under no parent. Keep them in "children", move or remove them first, or use patch. |\n| `multiple_parents` | yes | The op would list a node under a second parent (or twice in one): a node has one parent. Use move to change its parent. |\n| `cycle` | yes | The op would put a node inside its own subtree. |\n| `canvas_as_child` | yes | The op would place a canvas under a parent: canvases are only roots. |\n| `invalid_parent` | yes | A set or move under a parent that takes no children there: a type without children, or a slot the type does not have. |\n| `dangling_child` | yes | At the end of the stream, a child or slot id that names no node (it was pending and never arrived). |\n| `unreachable_node` | no (a warning) | At the end of the stream, a node no root reaches (nothing lists it as a child or slot). |\n| `pending_child` | no (a warning) | A child or slot id the op lists that has not arrived yet: accepted while streaming, an error (dangling_child) if it never comes. |\n| `capability_not_allowed` | yes | The op uses a node type, action, script, link, image host or navigation target the session\'s trust profile does not allow. |\n| `limit_exceeded` | yes | The op would exceed a limit of the trust profile (nodes, depth, text length, ops per call). |\n| `truncated_op` | yes | The stream ended inside an op: what arrived of it was not applied. |\n| `invalid_json` | yes | The tool input is not JSON where an op was expected. |\n| `invalid_variable` | yes | A vars op that declares a variable without a name, or sets one with a value its type cannot hold. |\n| `unknown_variable` | no (a warning) | A vars op that sets (or removes) a variable the scope does not declare: declare it with a full variable object. |\n| `malformed_input` | no (a warning) | The tool input\'s JSON was not well formed at a character offset (a stray or missing brace or bracket, a trailing or missing comma, an op that began before the previous one closed): the decoder repaired it and read the ops around it. Write balanced JSON: one closing brace per opening brace. |\n| `misplaced_field` | no (a warning) | A node field ("bind", "props", "actions"...) written on a set op instead of inside its "node": it was moved into the node. |\n\n(Continues: edit-ops.md#edit-ops-codes-of-the-edit-layer.)'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-the-tools', 'The tools', '`outline(document)` is one row per node, `[id, type, label, depth, {what is bound, states, triggers}]`, a sixth to\na seventh of the document\'s size (the two m05 pages: 6.7 KB for 147 nodes against 47.6 KB, 8.3 KB for 196 nodes\nagainst 55.4 KB). `readUi` answers the `ui_read` tool: the outline, or whole nodes by id.\n\nThe two tools (`uiTools(catalog)`), their input schemas generated from the op declarations and the catalog\'s\nnode types (the agent profile over the core: [ui_tools.json](ui_tools.json)):\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    "name": "ui_apply",\n    "description": "Build or change the UI. The UI is one document: a flat map of nodes by id, each a Flutter-like widget (type, layout, decoration, props, children). Ops apply in order: set (create or replace a node, optionally placing it under a parent), patch (RFC 7396 merge patch on one node; null deletes a key), move, remove, vars (set or declare variables) and roots (the screens). Ids are the addresses: choose short readable ids. A node may list children that arrive in later ops. When building, write top-down: the roots op and the canvas first, then each container before its children, section by section, so the UI paints as it streams. To change an existing UI, send only the small ops the change needs (patch a prop instead of re-sending a node). The result lists the ops that were rejected with a code, a path and a suggestion: fix and resend only those.",\n    "input_schema": "(see ui_tools.json)"\n  },\n  {\n    "name": "ui_read",\n    "description": "Read the current UI before changing it: the outline (one row per node: id, type, label, depth, what is bound), or full nodes by id. Read the outline first; ask for full nodes only for the ones you will change.",\n    "input_schema": "(see ui_tools.json)"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nA schema that enumerates every node type and key is the wrong shape for a runtime tool: it costs tokens on every\ncall and still cannot say what a prop holds. `uiTools(catalog, compact: true)` gives the op envelope strictly (op\nnames, field names and kinds from the declarations; [ui_tools.compact.json](ui_tools.compact.json)) and leaves the\nnode\'s contents to the system prompt: the **base** in its runtime form, `runtimeStarter(catalog:, profile:, kind:)`\n([RUNTIME_STARTER.md](RUNTIME_STARTER.md)). It is the same document as [STARTER.md](STARTER.md), assembled by\n`baseDocument` from the same generated segments, with the runtime envelope (answering rules, the ops, two examples\nthat apply cleanly) in place of the file envelope (the render command, sites as files), and its tables restricted\nto what the trust profile allows. `ui_read {"types": [...]}` describes any other allowed type on demand\n(`describeTypes`), and validation does the rest.\n\nThe base ends with the index of the third tool, `ui_docs {"topic": ...}` (`uiTools(catalog, compact: true, docs:\ntrue)`). `docsTopic` answers with these pages: a page\'s *When to use*, minimal example and pitfalls, and the list of\nits sections; any section by its anchor (`ui_docs "forms-validation-and-an-error-state"`); any node type, action\ntype, trigger or behavior by name, generated from the declarations. A link `forms.md#x` in the files is\n\n(Continues: edit-ops.md#edit-ops-the-tools.)'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-the-trust-profile', 'The trust profile', '`TrustProfile` is what an edited document may use: node types and action types (a restricted catalog,\n`profile.restrict(catalog)`), scripts, link and image hosts, navigation, and limits (nodes, depth, text length, ops\nper call). `TrustProfile.agent`, the default for agent-written UI, allows the core\'s node types and its local\nactions (variables, values, in-document navigation, modals, toasts, scrolling, animation, `componentEvent`): no\nscripts, no links, no host functions, no feature library\'s types. A violation is `capability_not_allowed` or\n`limit_exceeded`, with the op and the path.\n\n`componentEvent` in a document that is not a component (an agent\'s UI) is the event to the document\'s host: the\nsession delivers it to the app, which sends it to the model.'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-a-follow-up-edit', 'A follow-up edit', 'Then a follow-up instruction ("add a button under the title, drop the body text, make the title say Welcome") is\na few ops, not a new document:\n\n```json\n{"ops": [\n  {"op": "set", "id": "cta", "parent": "page", "index": 1,\n   "node": {"type": "row", "children": ["ctaText"], "layout": {"width": {"hug": 140}, "height": 44, "padding": [16, 0, 16, 0]},\n            "decoration": {"fills": [{"color": "#FF2563EB"}], "radius": 10}}},\n  {"op": "set", "id": "ctaText", "node": {"type": "text", "layout": {"width": {"hug": 100}, "height": {"hug": 20}},\n            "props": {"text": "Get started", "style": {"fontSize": 15, "fontWeight": 600, "color": "#FFFFFFFF"}}}},\n  {"op": "remove", "ids": ["body"]},\n  {"op": "patch", "id": "title", "patch": {"props": {"text": "Welcome"}}},\n  {"op": "vars", "scope": "home", "set": {"taps": {"type": "integer", "value": 0}}},\n  {"op": "patch", "id": "cta", "patch": {"actions": [{"trigger": {"type": "click"},\n            "actions": [{"type": "setVariable", "variable": "taps", "op": "add", "value": 1}]}]}},\n  {"op": "move", "id": "cta", "parent": "page", "index": 0}\n]}\n```\n\nA batch with a mistake: the second op misspells `fills`. It is rejected with a code, the path and a suggestion; the\nfirst applies:\n\n```json\n{\n  "ops": [\n    {"op": "patch", "id": "title", "patch": {"props": {"text": "Welcome back"}}},\n    {"op": "patch", "id": "title", "patch": {"decoration": {"fill": [{"color": "#FFF1F5F9"}]}}}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe result (`EditResult.toModelJson()`, the tool result):\n\n```json\n{\n  "rev": 1,\n  "applied": [0],\n  "ids": {"patched": ["title"]},\n  "rejected": [\n    {\n      "op": 1,\n      "code": "unknown_decoration_key",\n      "path": "/nodes/title/decoration/fill",\n      "message": "decoration has no \\"fill\\": a fill is a list of paints: \\"decoration\\": {\\"fills\\": [{\\"color\\": \\"#FF2563EB\\"}]}.",\n      "nodeId": "title",\n      "expected": [\n        "fills",\n        "strokes",\n        "strokeWidth",\n        "strokeAlign",\n        "strokeSides",\n        "strokeCap",\n        "dashPattern",\n        "radius",\n        "effects",\n        "clip",\n        "blendMode",\n        "foregroundFills",\n        "shape"\n      ],\n      "suggestion": "fills"\n    }\n  ],\n  "warnings": [],\n  "document": {"roots": ["home"], "nodes": 4},\n  "next": "The UI exists as described (rev 1): fix it by patching it, in one ui_apply call. Resend only the rejected ops, corrected, with the same ids; patch fields of nodes that exist. Never start over: no new roots, no new canvas, no re-sending nodes that applied."\n}\n```'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-streaming', 'Streaming', 'LLM APIs deliver a tool call\'s input as text deltas cut anywhere. `OpStreamDecoder` yields each op the moment its\nclosing brace arrives (inside a `set` with `nodes`, each node as its own brace closes), whatever the cuts; a stream\ncut mid-op keeps every complete op and reports the tail (`truncated_op`).\n\n**Malformed input.** With eager (fine-grained) tool input streaming a provider does not promise valid JSON, and\nmodels do not always write it: a recorded live generation closed one brace too many three times in 24 ops. The\ndecoder works around op boundaries, not the enclosing JSON: it keeps a repaired copy of the text (a closer that\ncloses nothing is dropped, a missing one is inferred, trailing commas are removed, missing commas inserted), reads an\nop object (`{"op": ...}`) at any depth outside another op (after an `ops` array closed early, in a wrapper of\nanother name, after text), and when an op begins before the previous one closed, closes that one where the next\nbegan and reads what it can of it. Each repair is one `malformed_input` warning with the character offset; the ops\naround it apply. A node field written beside `"node"` (what an early closing brace produces) is moved into the node\n(`misplaced_field`). A single stray or missing brace, bracket or comma loses at most the op it is in\n(`test/edit_stream_recovery_test.dart` checks this property on 2,000 random edits, and that every chunking yields the\nsame ops). `OpStreamEnd.repairs` and `.recovered` count the repairs and the ops read after the first one.\n\nWhile a document streams in, it is incomplete by nature:\n\n* **Order.** Root first, then top-down: the `roots` op, the canvas, its container, then each section complete before\n  the next. Nothing breaks if a model does otherwise.\n* **Forward references.** A child id listed before its node arrives is **pending**, not an error; a reference to a\n  node, screen or variable that has not arrived yet (a state that patches it, a navigation to it) is a warning while\n  the stream runs (`applyOps(..., incomplete: true)`). When the stream ends, `endOfStream` reports every id still\n  dangling (`dangling_child`), so the model can send it.\n* **Rendering** (the SDK\'s `UiSession`): nothing paints until a root canvas has content; a pending child takes no\n  room (or the size a `set`\'s `placeholders` gave it); a container none of whose children has arrived waits for\n  its first, so no empty shells flash; text and values are never shown half-written (an op applies only whole).'),
      DocSection('edit-ops-the-retry-protocol', 'The retry protocol', 'After a stream, the tool result (`EditResult.toModelJson()`) says exactly what the document holds and what to do:\n`rev`, the ops that applied (by index, and the ids they set, patched, moved and removed), every rejected op with its\ncode, path, what was expected and a suggestion, warnings (at most 20), the pending ids, the document\'s roots and node\ncount, and, when anything was rejected or is pending, `next`: fix it by patching the document as it is (same ids),\nnever start over. A host sends it back and lets the model answer with one more `ui_apply`, a capped number of\ntimes (the chat example: 2), stopping when nothing is rejected or pending. `applyOps(..., protectRoots: true)`\nanswers a batch that would replace the existing roots or create a new canvas with `start_over` (a live retry did\nexactly that instead of fixing three ops). The diagnostics models hit carry the fix as JSON (`explainDiagnostic`): a\nsize written as a layout key (`"height": {"hug": 46}`), children on a type that takes none (the types that do, and a\nrow with a fill for a tag or button), a button\'s `text` (its `label`). `uiLints(document)` adds what no declaration\ncatches: `empty_button` (a tappable box that shows nothing) and `low_contrast` (text in its background\'s colour); a\nrenderer adds `missing_glyph` and `no_size` (the SDK\'s `UiSession.renderDiagnostics`).'),
    ],
  ),
  DocPage(
    id: 'render',
    title: 'The render command: checking your work',
    summary: 'Checking a document or a site before trusting it: strict validation, screenshots at each size, the states after taps, typing and scrolling, and a diagnostic for every problem with a code, a JSON pointer and what to do.',
    when: 'Checking a document or a site before trusting it: strict validation, screenshots at each size, the states after\ntaps, typing and scrolling, and a diagnostic for every problem with a code, a JSON pointer and what to do. The\ncommand lives in the SDK repository (`tool/render/render.sh`; its README has every option).',
    example: 'The smallest document, and the command that checks it at three sizes:\n\n```json\n{\n  "schema": 3,\n  "roots": ["home"],\n  "nodes": {\n    "home": {\n      "type": "canvas",\n      "name": "Home",\n      "slots": {"body": "page"},\n      "layout": {"width": 390, "height": 844},\n      "decoration": {"fills": [{"color": "#FFFFFFFF"}]}\n    },\n    "page": {\n      "type": "column",\n      "children": ["title", "body"],\n      "layout": {"width": {"fill": 390}, "height": {"fill": 844}, "padding": 24},\n      "props": {"crossAxisAlignment": "start", "spacing": 12}\n    },\n    "title": {\n      "type": "text",\n      "layout": {"width": {"fill": 342}, "height": {"hug": 34}},\n      "props": {"text": "Hello", "style": {"fontSize": 28, "fontWeight": 700, "color": "#FF111827"}}\n    },\n    "body": {\n      "type": "text",\n      "layout": {"width": {"fill": 342}, "height": {"hug": 40}},\n      "props": {\n        "text": "A column with two texts, filling the screen\'s width.",\n        "style": {"fontSize": 15, "height": 1.4, "color": "#FF4B5563"}\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```sh\ntool/render/render.sh home.json --out shots/ --sizes 390x844,834x1112,1440x900 --full-page\n```',
    pitfalls: '- Every diagnostic is about the page as it renders at that size: its media rules applied, what scrolls there, what\n  an ancestor clips away.\n- The command does not fetch network images or fonts: put the files beside `site.json` and name them by their\n  relative path.\n- A `--tap` scrolls the target into view; a `--full-page` capture still starts at the top.\n- Runs on one SDK checkout take turns (each waits for the one before): start several, and they finish one after\n  another.',
    words: 2881,
    items: [],
    sections: [
      DocSection('render-what-it-does', 'What it does', 'Decoding is lenient: a misspelled key, a value of the wrong shape or an unknown enum value is silently ignored or\nreplaced by its default. Never trust a document that has not been checked. The command validates every document\nstrictly, renders each screen with the SDK at each size, writes `shots/<screen>@<w>x<h>.png` and\n`shots/diagnostics.json`, and prints one line per problem with a code, the JSON pointer of the value\n(`/nodes/hero-title/props/style/fontSize`) or the node, and what to do. It exits 1 when there are errors.'),
      DocSection('render-steps-before-the-capture', 'Steps before the capture', 'In order: `--tap <id>` (`<id>#<n>` for the n-th copy of a list item; a node out of view or under an overlay is\nscrolled to the middle first, and a tap that would land on another node is reported, not made), `--hover <id>`,\n`--focus <id>`, `--unfocus`, `--type <id>=<text>`, `--key <key>`, `--set <variable>=<json>`, `--scroll <id or\nscreen>=<px>`, `--drag <id>=<dx>,<dy>` (or `=<other id>`: onto a node), `--navigate <screen>`, `--wait <ms>`. A step\nmay name a node by its `name` too. In a site, steps name nodes inside components by path (`--tap plans/card1/cta`)\nand a node in a list\'s copy as `<template>#<n>/<node>` (`evCard#0/evReserve`, the same as `evReserve#0`). Ids are\nper document, so a page, a component and an open modal may share one: a step naming an id two shown nodes have is\nan error listing how to name each (`page/saved`, the page\'s own under its canvas id; `footer/saved`, the\ncomponent\'s). A step on a node hidden at one size (a media rule, another breakpoint\'s canvas) is skipped there with\na note; the others run. `--navigate` takes a canvas id, a screen name or a URL path, with a query string for the\nparams the screen reads as `\${route.<name>}` (`--navigate speaker?id=ada`, `--navigate /project?id=7`); later steps\nact on the screen navigated to. A `--set` holds: a timer or `load` reaction that writes the variable afterwards does\nnot change the capture (`set_overwritten` says it happened); `--now <ISO time>` pins `now()`. A tap, a drag or a\nnavigation first unfocuses a focused text field elsewhere, as a click outside it does. `--label <name>` keeps the screenshots and\n`diagnostics-<name>.json` of each state apart. After each step the page settles before the next one and the capture\n(about a second): an animation shorter than that is over when the screenshot is taken; to see one mid-flight,\nlengthen its duration in a copy. `--key` presses a key for the screen\'s `keyDown` reactions; it does not submit a\nfocused text field (test `submitted` chains through a button). In nested lists `#n` counts the copies across the\nwhole repeat, outer item by outer item, from 0: with a 7-day grid per habit, `day#9` is the second habit\'s third\nday. `--full-page` captures the whole page from its top, its own scroll\nheight, as a reader sees it: content fixed to the window and an open modal (with its dimmed backdrop) in the first\nwindow, a sticky node once in its place, and after a navigation the page navigated to at its own height; without it\nthe capture is the window where the steps left the page. `--theme light|dark` starts the site in that theme mode\n(PNG names end `-light`, `-dark`; a `--tap` on a `setThemeMode` switch then changes it). A site\'s declared fonts\n(`site.json`\'s `fonts`) and images named by a path relative to the site are read from its folder with no flag;\n`--fonts <dir>` adds other font files (`--fonts system`: the system\'s). `--component <Type>` and\n`--components-all` render components alone.'),
      DocSection('render-validation-codes', 'Validation codes', 'From `package:codelessly_api/validate.dart`:\n\n| Code | Severity | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `invalid_document` | error | The document is not a JSON object, or a top-level key has the wrong shape (nodes not an object, roots not a list). |\n| `schema_version` | warning | The document has no "schema", or one this package does not read (it reads 2 and 3, and writes 3). |\n| `schema_outdated` | info | A schema-2 document: it loads, migrated to schema 3 in memory (`migrate2to3`; `dart run tool/migrate.dart` rewrites the file). The other diagnostics are of the migrated document. |\n| `legacy_value` | warning | A `legacy.<name>` key the migration kept: a value that had no current form and is no longer read. |\n| `unknown_document_key` | error | A top-level key of the document that nothing reads. |\n| `dangling_root` | error | A root id that names no node. |\n| `root_not_canvas` | warning | A root that is not a canvas: it is never shown as a screen. |\n| `no_roots` | error | The document has no roots, so nothing is shown. |\n| `invalid_node` | error | A node that is not an object or has no "type": decoding drops it. |\n| `unknown_node_type` | error | A node type no declaration knows: it renders as an empty box. |\n| `unknown_node_key` | error | A key on a node that is neither a node field nor a behavior: it is kept but does nothing. |\n| `unknown_prop` | error | A key in "props" that the node type does not declare: it does nothing. |\n| `unknown_layout_key` | error | A key in "layout" that layout does not read. |\n| `unknown_decoration_key` | error | A key in "decoration" that decoration does not read. |\n| `unknown_field` | error | A key inside a value (a paint, a text style, a behavior model...) that its type does not read. |\n| `wrong_type` | error | A value of the wrong JSON type or shape: decoding ignores it and uses the default. |\n| `loose_value` | warning | A value of another JSON type that decoding converts (the text "12" for a number): write the type itself. |\n| `invalid_enum` | error | A value that is not one of the allowed names: decoding uses the default. |\n| `out_of_range` | error | A number outside its declared bounds: decoding clamps it. |\n| `decoration_ignored` | warning | Fills, strokes, radius or shadows on a type without a box decoration: only blur effects are kept. |\n| `dangling_child` | error | A child id that names no node. |\n| `dangling_slot` | error | A slot whose id names no node. |\n| `multiple_parents` | error | A node listed as a child (or slot) of more than one parent, or twice in one parent. |\n| `cycle` | error | A node that contains itself. |\n| `unreachable_node` | warning | A node no root reaches: it is never shown. |\n| `unknown_slot` | error | A slot name the node type does not have. |\n| `children_not_allowed` | error | A "children" list on a type that takes none (or takes slots only): they are never shown. |\n| `slots_not_allowed` | error | A "slots" map on a type that has no slots. |\n| `canvas_as_child` | error | A canvas used as the child of another node: canvases are screens, only roots. |\n| `layout_field_ignored` | warning | A layout field the node\'s parent does not read (x/y outside a stack, flex/align outside a row or column). |\n\n(Continues: render.md#render-validation-codes.)'),
      DocSection('render-render-codes', 'Render codes', 'The render step adds what only rendering shows: `render_exception` (naming the node), `action_error`,\n`interaction_failed`, `tap_no_reaction`, `layout_overflow`, `offscreen`, `text_clipped`, `text_truncated`,\n`text_no_color`, `missing_glyph`, `icon_not_drawn`, `expression_not_evaluated`, `path_overflow`, `font_fallback`,\n`font_missing`, `fill_fallback`, `image_failed`, `image_network`, `empty_screen`, `invalid_json`,\n`component_missing`, `set_overwritten`. Links to a screen that is missing are one `unknown_screen` per screen, with\nhow many links name it. Formulas are\nparsed by the SDK\'s own parser there, so formula syntax errors and unknown names in formulas are reported only by\nthe render command.'),
      DocSection('render-a-loop-that-converges', 'A loop that converges', 'Validate and render after each section, read the diagnostics, then look at the PNGs next to the target: validation\ncannot tell you that a page is ugly. Fix by the JSON pointer each line gives. Capture each state with its own\n`--label` so the files do not overwrite each other.'),
      DocSection('render-capturing-states', 'Capturing states', 'The annual price: `--tap billingSwitch --label annual`. An open menu at phone width: `--sizes 390x844 --tap\nmenuButton --label menu`. An error state: `--type seats=0 --unfocus --label error`. A value set directly:\n`--set annual=true`. A detail page with its parameter: `--navigate speaker?id=ada --full-page`. A reordered list or\na slider: `--drag handle#0=0,120`, `--drag volume=80,0`. A countdown before the event: `--now\n2026-11-13T09:00:00Z`.'),
      DocSection('render-what-a-run-keeps', 'What a run keeps', 'Storage, variables and the clipboard live in memory for one run: steps and `--navigate` see what earlier steps\nwrote, and each size starts afresh, as does each run (nothing persists between runs, so a "restored on reload"\nstate is captured by writing it first: `--tap save --navigate home`). `downloadFile` saves its file under\n`<out>/downloads/<capture>/` and `copyToClipboard` copies in memory; both are listed in the output and in the\ndiagnostics file (`downloads`, `clipboard`). `openLink` opens nothing.'),
      DocSection('render-comparing-with-a-reference', 'Comparing with a reference', '`--compare <dir>` picks, for each capture, the PNG of the same size whose name is the screen and the state exactly\n(`section--saved--390x844.png` for `--label saved`, never `section--saved-empty`); with no `--label`, `default` or\nno state. When the reference names a page otherwise, map it: `--compare-map home=index`.'),
    ],
  ),
  DocPage(
    id: 'values',
    title: 'Value types, models and enums',
    summary: '',
    when: 'Looking up how one value is written: a value type (a colour, an alignment, insets), a model no feature page shows\n(its fields) or an enum (its names). Link to one with `values.md#type-<Name>` or `values.md#enum-<Name>`. Models\nthat belong to a feature are on that feature\'s page (TextStyle on the theme page, InputDecoration on the forms\npage...); the [A–Z](index.md#index-a-z) finds any of them.',
    example: 'Value types are written in a compact form of their own; models are objects; enums are names:\n\n```json\n{"color": "#FF2563EB", "padding": [16, 8, 16, 8], "alignment": [0, -1], "fit": "cover"}\n```',
